    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f871e802_25b52739",
        "filename": "system/bta/le_audio/audio_hal_client/audio_source_hal_asrc.h",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 44,
      "author": {
        "id": 1899057
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-17T18:02:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "By default C/C++, cast every operands to `int` whenever it\u0027s bigger than the type of the variable. About `uint32_t`, if the `int` comes to be backed in 64 bits, it can leads to bad beahaviors of some computation. Especially, in a case operation can be made unsigned when `uint32_t` is backed to `unsigned int`, instead of doing an implicit conversion to `int`. For example uint32_val + 1, the `1` can be casted implicitly to `unsigned int` or `int`.\nWhenever the context does not give much information, I should recommend to use `int` as an integer when the precision is not a concern.",
      "parentUuid": "f78eca0e_230f6b31",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 44,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 44,
        "endChar": 7
      },
      "revId": "928ba263ac007aca744b381b1c4c28f5fc58e8b3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
